From 07062ead916601fd125dd8f570ef959b01f90383 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 23:22:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add a global render-resolution scale
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Profiling on a weak iGPU (Iris Xe) showed Forward and Prepass
dominating frame time despite trivial geometry (13 draw calls, ~2M
vertices) — the cost is per-fragment shading at native resolution, not
draw-call/batching overhead. GTAO/SSR/planar were already partly
scaled down, but the two biggest passes (Forward, Prepass) and
everything else in the scene chain (HDR target, bloom, autoexposure,
TAA) still ran at full native resolution with no way to trade
sharpness for fill-rate.

Adds RenderConfig.RenderScale (default 1, identical to today's
behavior): the HDR target, prepass, GTAO, SSR, bloom, autoexposure,
TAA, clouds, and planar reflections all render at this fraction of the
window's real framebuffer size. Only the final tonemap draw stays
pinned to native resolution — it already samples the scene through a
linear-filtered sampler into a full-size viewport (see HDRFramebuffer/
BloomPass/SSRPass/TAAPass, all already linear-filtered), so upscaling
a smaller render falls out of that existing draw for free; no
dedicated upscale pass was needed. Tunable at runtime via the new
"Render Scale" slider in the Viewport inspector section
(RenderingSystem.CheckRenderScale re-derives and re-applies the scaled
size once a frame when it changes).

Incidental fix: RenderingSystem.Resize never called
PlanarReflectionPass.Resize, so its target silently stayed pinned to
whatever size InitializeComponents saw at startup after any real
window resize — this surfaced because RenderScale changes now share
the same Resize() path a window resize takes.

Verified with headless renders at scale=1 (matches prior output) and
scale=0.5 (renders correctly, upscales cleanly via existing linear
filtering, no corruption/crashes/incomplete-framebuffer errors).
Absolute frame-time numbers from this environment's software
rasterizer aren't representative of a real GPU's fill-rate-bound
behavior, so the actual speedup needs measuring on real hardware.
